	(text_box "WHAT THE GATE DOES BEFORE SOFTWARE WRITES THE DAC.\nThe 100k holds all three gates down whenever the DAC is not driving them, which covers an unpopulated or unpowered DAC and\nthe moment before the first I2C write. It does NOT cover the DAC powering up from its own EEPROM into something other than\nzero - and half scale is exactly the point of MAXIMUM pass-element dissipation, 0.8 W in each of three SOT-223s.\nCONFIRMED in round 28 from the MCP4725 datasheet, Table 5-3: the factory EEPROM is PD = 00 with D11 = 1, i.e. MID-SCALE,\n1.65 V on the gates from the first power-up.  So the fix is procedural and it is the FIRST thing bringup.py does: Write-\nDAC-and-EEPROM = 0 with PD = 01 (1k pull-down), after which the part powers up off every time.  Do not connect the 15 V\njack to a board on which that has not yet run once."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 299.72 246.38 0)
		(size 275.59 24.13)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "38aa8b0f-35e5-2e88-96e2-4bd2a2e33c5f")
	)
	(text_box "WHY EIGHT, AND WHY ON THE BOARD.\nEvery threshold on this board moves with temperature, and the movement is meant to be common mode because all\nthirteen channels share one rail and one die family.  Common mode is a claim, not a fact, and these eight sensors\nare how it gets tested: four corners give the board's gradient, and the four in the middle sit on the parts that\nactually dissipate - the fan-out cluster, the two LDOs, and the squarers at each end of the channel row."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 19.05 162.56 0)
		(size 265.43 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "58519797-7b50-eab2-2b4c-7e558573a33e")
	)
	(text_box "THE ADDRESS MAP IS THE POSITION MAP.\nA2:A0 are strapped to the binary index, so the part at 0x48 is the leftmost here and the leftmost on the board, and the\nsoftware never needs a lookup table telling it which sensor is where.  The eight span 0x48-0x4F, which is the whole TMP1075\naddress space: there is no room for a ninth, and that is the reason the DS18B20 probes hang off a 1-Wire bridge instead of\nbeing more of these."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 293.37 162.56 0)
		(size 284.48 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "58eb11e3-5283-37ce-4c21-69d2bb15f8bc")
	)
	(text_box "WHY A DAC AND A LINEAR PASS FET, AND NOT PWM.\nPWM is the cheap way to drive a heater and it is the wrong way here.  Switching amperes of heater current a few hundred\ntimes a second puts that fundamental and its harmonics into the ground plane and into the supply, and this board's whole\njob is measuring picoseconds of phase.  A 12-bit DAC into a linear pass FET dissipates more and radiates nothing.  The\nloop itself is Python on the PYNQ: read a TMP1075, write the DAC, integrate."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 299.72 215.9 0)
		(size 275.59 15.24)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "72b11ec3-43e0-c768-8376-a8ca0ec2935b")
	)
	(text_box "A ground on every even pin, so a scope probe's spring ground reaches the signal\nbeside it without a clip lead - which would otherwise show you its own loop\nrather than the signal.\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: any beat, pps or clock.  This header sits at the\nfar edge, so tapping one would hang a 150 mm unterminated stub on a 2 ns edge\nand make those channels behave differently from the rest.  Probe them at their\nown TP_BEAT and TP_PPS instead, where the ground return is millimetres long."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 199.39 341.63 0)
		(size 121.92 21.59)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "7413465d-eed9-db49-03a1-c0a24c472100")
	)
	(text_box "12-bit DAC straight onto the pass FET's gate.  The temperature loop runs in\nPython on the PYNQ: read a TMP1075, write the DAC, integrate.  Nothing switches,\nand it costs no header pins because it shares the I2C bus with the thermometers."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 160.02 218.44 0)
		(size 121.92 10.16)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "f0466923-e264-e53d-1731-62a0fe21fd18")
	)
